%******************************************************************************
\chapter{Introduction}
%******************************************************************************

\section{Motivations}

%claude:
The goal of this book is to present with full details the source code of
a small but complete relational database management system, so that the
reader can see how a SQL query actually turns into bytes read from and
written to a file on disk.

Here are a few questions I hope this book will answer:
\begin{itemize}
\item How does a SQL query get parsed into an abstract syntax tree,
  and how does that tree get compiled into something executable?
\item What does the executable form of a query actually look like?
  What is a database virtual machine and why use bytecode at all,
  rather than interpreting the query tree directly?
\item How are rows and indexes actually laid out on disk, and how
  does the B-Tree layer make lookups, range scans, and insertions
  efficient?
\item How does a query optimizer decide when it is safe to use an
  index instead of a full table scan?
\end{itemize}

\section{[[chidb]]}

%claude:
I chose [[chidb]] for this book because, unlike a production database
like SQLite or Postgres, it was designed from the start to be read and
extended by students: the SQL front end, pager, and B-Tree layer are
given, and the reader (originally a student in a course) implements the
database virtual machine, the query optimizer, and the bytecode
generator. That makes it a rare thing: a real, working RDBMS small
enough to fully explain in one book, whose layering mirrors SQLite's
closely enough that lessons here transfer directly.

\section{Other databases considered}

%claude:
SQLite itself was the obvious alternative, but its production-grade
optimizations (WAL mode, a cost-based query planner, generated VDBE
opcodes) would have made for a much longer, less didactic book.
[[chidb]] keeps the same architecture --- SQL text to AST to bytecode
to B-Tree to pager to file --- at a scale a single chapter set can
cover completely.

