\section{[[chidb_step]] and [[chidb_finalize]]}

%******************************************************************************
\chapter{The SQL front end: [[chisql]]}
%******************************************************************************

%claude:
[[chisql]] is the library that turns SQL text into an in-memory AST:
a flex/bison grammar producing the statement, table, column, condition,
and literal types the rest of the pipeline consumes.

%******************************************************************************
\chapter{The query optimizer}
%******************************************************************************

%claude:
[[chidb_stmt_optimize]] rewrites a parsed statement before code
generation, most notably pushing single-table conditions down next to
the table they filter (``sigma-pushing'') so that the code generator
can turn them into index seeks instead of full scans.

%******************************************************************************
\chapter{Code generation}
%******************************************************************************

%claude:
[[chidb_stmt_codegen]] compiles an (optimized) statement into an array
of DBM bytecode instructions: one sequence of opcodes per statement
kind (CREATE TABLE, CREATE INDEX, INSERT, SELECT, ...), with indexed
columns compiling to seeks and unindexed ones to scans with a filter.

%******************************************************************************
\chapter{The database virtual machine}
%******************************************************************************

%claude:
The DBM executes bytecode with a classic fetch-decode-execute loop; one
function per opcode handles the actual work (comparisons, cursor
movement, row assembly, ...) against table/index cursors.

%******************************************************************************
\chapter{B-Trees}
%******************************************************************************

%claude:
The B-Tree layer implements the on-disk B-Trees --- one per table or
index --- that back every cursor operation: find, insert, split, and
the traversal that a full scan or a range seek walks.

%******************************************************************************
\chapter{The pager}
%******************************************************************************

%claude:
The pager is the bottom of the stack: it turns page numbers into reads
and writes against the [[chidb]] file, and is the only layer that
touches the file directly.

%******************************************************************************
\chapter{The interactive shell}
%******************************************************************************

%claude:
The [[chidb]] shell is a small REPL built on top of the public
[[chidb_open]]/[[chidb_prepare]]/[[chidb_step]]/[[chidb_finalize]] API,
useful both as a demo and as a way to inspect compiled bytecode with
its \texttt{.explain} command.

